NAME
       id - print a user's user ID, group IDs and SELinux context

SYNOPSIS
       id [OPTION]... [USER]

DESCRIPTION
       With no USER, id shows the identity of the current shell: its user ID
       (uid), primary group (gid), every group it belongs to, and on an
       SELinux system its security context. With a USER name (or number) it
       shows that user's IDs and groups as they are in /etc/passwd and
       /etc/group right now.

       A shell keeps the groups it had when it started: after usermod -aG,
       "id student" shows the new group but student's open shell does not
       until student logs in again.

OPTIONS
       -u, --user
              Print only the user ID.

       -g, --group
              Print only the primary group ID.

       -G, --groups
              Print all group IDs, primary first.

       -n, --name
              With -u, -g or -G: print names instead of numbers.

       -Z, --context
              Print only the SELinux context of the current shell.

EXAMPLES
              $ id student
              uid=1000(student) gid=1000(student) groups=1000(student),10(wheel)
              $ id -un
              student
              $ id -nG student
              student wheel
              # id -u harry || echo "no user harry"

SEE ALSO
       groups(1), whoami(1), getent(1), useradd(8), usermod(8)
